    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn production inference stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang, DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron-based serving), TP and EP are frequently combined: dense/attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (sometimes with TP *within* an expert, i.e., EP\u00d7TP). This note covers the mechanics of both, their data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention projections: $W_Q, W_K, W_V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$, output projection $W_O \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$\n- MLP: up/gate projections $W_{up}, W_{gate} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times 4d}$, down projection $W_{down} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4d \\times d}$\n\nWith TP degree $T$ (world size $T$ devices):\n\n- **Column-parallel (weight-stationary split by output dimension)**: $W_Q, W_K, W_V$, $W_{up}$, $W",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
